This chapter describes how the library states and proves Borchers' theorem, its corollary for the
modular group of a wedge algebra, and the Bisognano–Wichmann theorem up to an internal symmetry.
It corresponds to
[Section 10.11](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#sctn:borchers-wedges)
of the blueprint. It joins the two preceding chapters: the {ref "tomita-takesaki"}[Tomita–Takesaki
chapter] supplies the modular unitary group $`\Delta^{it}` and the modular conjugation $`J` of a
von Neumann algebra with a cyclic and separating vector, and the {ref "reeh-schlieder"}[Reeh–Schlieder
chapter] supplies the cyclicity of the vacuum for local algebras, from which the vacuum is cyclic and
separating for the algebra of a wedge. The setting is Minkowski spacetime only.

# Overview
%%%
tag := "borchers-wedges-overview"
%%%

Tomita–Takesaki theory attaches to every pair $`(\mathcal{M}, \Omega)` of a von Neumann algebra and
a cyclic and separating vector a one-parameter unitary group $`\Delta^{it}` and an antiunitary
involution $`J`, defined purely algebraically. The Bisognano–Wichmann theorem says that for the
algebra of a wedge in the vacuum representation these objects are geometric. For the right wedge
$`W_R = \{x \mid |x^0| < x^1\}`, $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it}` is the unitary of the Lorentz boost of
rapidity $`-2\pi t` in the $`(x^0, x^1)`-plane, and $`J_{W_R}` is the CPT operator composed with a
rotation by $`\pi`. Two consequences make the theorem central to the subject. Restricted to a wedge,
the vacuum is a KMS state for the boost dynamics at inverse temperature $`2\pi` in the rapidity
variable: a uniformly accelerated observer sees the vacuum as a thermal state, the Unruh effect.
And the spacetime symmetry can be reconstructed from the net and the vacuum alone, the programme
of _modular geometry_.

Bisognano and Wichmann proved the theorem for nets generated by Wightman fields. For general
Haag–Kastler nets it is false: there are covariant nets with positive energy and a vacuum in which
the wedge modular group is not the boost group (J. Yngvason, "A note on essential duality",
_Lett. Math. Phys._ 31 (1994) 127–141). The library has no Wightman fields, and it formalizes the
part of the theory that holds in the Haag–Kastler setting, in three steps.

* _Borchers' theorem_ (H.-J. Borchers, _Commun. Math. Phys._ 143 (1992) 315–332, Theorem II.9).
  If a unitary group $`V(t)` with positive generator fixes a cyclic and separating vector
  $`\Omega` of a von Neumann algebra $`\mathcal{M}` and maps $`\mathcal{M}` into itself for
  $`t \ge 0`, then
  $$`\Delta^{is}V(t)\Delta^{-is} = V(e^{-2\pi s}t), \qquad JV(t)J = V(-t).`
  This is pure operator algebra and involves no net.
* _The wedge corollary._ Applied to the algebra of the right wedge and the lightlike translations
  along its edges, Borchers' theorem fixes how the wedge modular objects act on every translation:
  $`\Delta_{W_R}^{is}U(a)\Delta_{W_R}^{-is} = U(\Lambda(-2\pi s)a)`, as the boost does, and
  $`J_{W_R}U(a)J_{W_R} = U(ja)`, with $`j` the reflection of the $`(x^0, x^1)`-plane. The unitary
  $`Z(t) = \Delta_{W_R}^{it}U(b(2\pi t))` then measures how far $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it}` is from the
  boost $`U(b(-2\pi t))`; Bisognano–Wichmann is the statement $`Z \equiv \mathbf{1}`.
* _Bisognano–Wichmann up to an internal symmetry (BW-a)._ Under _modular covariance_ (MC) of the
  right wedge, that $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it}` moves the local algebras as the boosts do,
  $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it} = Z(t)\,U(b(-2\pi t))` with $`Z(t)` a unitary that fixes $`\Omega`, maps
  every local algebra onto itself, commutes with the translations, the boosts of $`W_R` and every
  wedge modular object, and depends on $`t` as a strongly continuous one-parameter group.

Exact Bisognano–Wichmann for the canonical representation of the Poincaré cover generated by the
wedge modular groups is the theorem of Brunetti, Guido and Longo (_Rev. Math. Phys._ 7 (1995)
57–71, arXiv:funct-an/9401002, Theorem 2.3). Its proof rests on the measurable cohomology of
Polish group extensions, and it is not formalized; BW-a records it in a `**Restriction:**` line.
In nets with a global gauge group, $`Z(t)` may lie in that group, so BW-a excludes no theory with
internal symmetries.

Every statement of Section 10.11 is proved in Lean, with no `sorry`. Borchers' theorem is in the
namespace `Physicslib4.Modular`, the wedges, region algebras and covariant vacuum representations
in `Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler`, and the strip lemma and the causal-cone lemmas in
`Physicslib4`.

# Design
%%%
tag := "borchers-wedges-design"
%%%

The conventions of the {ref "haag-kastler-design"}[Minkowski chapter] and the
{ref "tomita-takesaki-design"}[Tomita–Takesaki chapter] carry over. Modular objects are formed
with a reference conjugation `C`, an antiunitary involution, on which they do not depend; every
statement below takes `C` and `hC : ∀ x, C (C x) = x` as arguments.

## Wedges are derived regions

The net axioms are indexed by diamonds only, and wedges are not added to them. A wedge is a set
of spacetime points: the right wedge $`W_R = \{x \mid |x^0| < x^1\}` (`rightWedge`), the left
wedge $`W_L = \{x \mid x^1 < -|x^0|\}` (`leftWedge`), and in general any $`gW_R` with $`g` in
the restricted Poincaré group, the predicate `IsWedge`. A wedge indexes a von Neumann algebra only
in a representation, through the algebra of a region.

## The algebra of a region

For a \*-representation $`\pi` and an arbitrary set $`\mathbf{O}` of spacetime points, the
library defines
$$`R(\mathbf{O}) = \Big(\bigcup_{\mathbf{B} \subseteq \mathbf{O}} R(\mathbf{B})\Big)'',`
the union over diamonds $`\mathbf{B}` contained in $`\mathbf{O}`, with $`R(\mathbf{B})` the local
von Neumann algebra of the Minkowski chapter. The inclusion $`\mathbf{B} \subseteq \mathbf{O}` is
plain inclusion, so on a diamond the region algebra agrees with the local von Neumann algebra
(`regionVonNeumann_of_isAlexandrovBasisSet`), and no closure condition on $`\mathbf{B}` is needed.
A region containing no nonempty diamond gets $`\mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}`. The region algebra is a
derived object, exists only in a representation, and changes no axiom. Locality carries over
from diamonds to regions whose diamonds are pairwise completely spacelike, and in particular to
opposite wedges: $`W_R` and $`W_L` are completely spacelike by a coordinate criterion,
$`p^1 - q^1 > |p^0| + |q^0|`, which rests on the fact that causal precedence places the
displacement in the closed forward cone (`standardMinkowski_causallyPrecedes_sub_mem_closedForwardCone`).

## Covariant vacuum representations are a hypothesis

The vacuum data of the Minkowski chapter and the Reeh–Schlieder theorem implement the Poincaré
action only on the vectors $`\pi(a)\Omega` and require positive energy only along
future-timelike translations. Borchers' theorem and its wedge corollary need more: operator-level
implementation $`U(g)\pi(a)U(g)^{-1} = \pi(\beta_g a)`, the group law, invariance of $`\Omega`,
continuity along boosts and translations, and positive energy along the lightlike translations
$`U(t\,l_\pm)` that run along the edges of the wedge. The structure `IsCovariantVacuumRep`
collects these hypotheses. It is passed as a hypothesis, as the vacuum data are. Its docstring
carries two `**Restriction:**` lines:

* continuity is required only along continuous one-parameter subgroups of the Poincaré group,
  which is what the proofs use (the boost flows and the translation flows); the intended form is
  strong continuity on the whole group, which the GNS construction of an invariant state with
  continuous matrix coefficients supplies;
* positive energy is assumed along lightlike directions too; the intended form requires it only
  along future-timelike translations, with the lightlike case derived by a strong-resolvent limit
  of the generators, which is not formalized.

Weak additivity in the von Neumann sense, from the {ref "reeh-schlieder"}[Reeh–Schlieder chapter],
is the further hypothesis under which $`\Omega` is cyclic and separating for every wedge algebra
(`IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_isCyclicSeparatingVector`). The later statements take that
standardness as a proof `hΩ`, which names the modular objects, rather than weak additivity itself.

## The boost convention

The boost matrix `boostMatrix s`, written $`\Lambda(s)`, acts on the $`(x^0, x^1)`-plane with
rapidity $`s`, and its sign is fixed by $`\Lambda(s)l_+ = e^{s}l_+` and
$`\Lambda(s)l_- = e^{-s}l_-` for $`l_\pm = (1, \pm 1, 0, 0)`. The boost of the right wedge is
$`b(s) = (\Lambda(s), 0)` (`wedgeBoost`), and the boost of $`gW_R` along $`g` is
$`b_g(s) = g\,b(s)\,g^{-1}` (`wedgeBoostAlong`), indexed by $`g` rather than by the wedge. With this
convention the wedge modular group corresponds to $`b(-2\pi t)`. Brunetti, Guido and Longo write
$`\Lambda_{W_1}(t)` for the boost with matrix rows $`(\cosh 2\pi t, -\sinh 2\pi t)` and
$`(-\sinh 2\pi t, \cosh 2\pi t)`, so their $`\Lambda_{W_1}(t)` is the library's
$`\Lambda(-2\pi t)`, and (MC) below is their Assumption 2.1 for the single wedge $`W_R`.

## The objects of BW-a

* `IsHalfSidedTranslation M Ω V`: the hypothesis of Borchers' theorem, positive energy of $`V`
  (as the parent structure `IsPositiveEnergy V`), $`V(t)\Omega = \Omega`, and
  $`V(t)\mathcal{M}V(t)^* \subseteq \mathcal{M}` for $`t \ge 0`. It does not require $`\Omega`
  to be cyclic or separating; the theorem assumes that separately.
* `wedgeReflection`: the linear map $`j(x^0, x^1, x^2, x^3) = (-x^0, -x^1, x^2, x^3)`. It is
  proper but not orthochronous, so $`(j, 0)` is not in the restricted Poincaré group and $`j` acts
  only on translation vectors.
* `wedgeModularDefect`: the family $`Z(t) = \Delta_{W_R}^{it}U(b(2\pi t))`.
* `IsWedgeModularCovariant`: (MC), the equality of sets of operators
  $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it}R(\mathbf{B})\Delta_{W_R}^{-it} = R(b(-2\pi t)\mathbf{B})` for every diamond
  $`\mathbf{B}`. It does not involve $`U` and does not assert $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it} = U(b(-2\pi t))`.
* `IsInternalSymmetry`: a unitary that fixes $`\Omega` and maps every local von Neumann algebra
  onto itself. Commutation with the translations is not part of the definition; BW-a states it
  separately.

## One analytic restriction

Borchers' proof writes the powers $`\Delta^{-i\zeta}` on the strip $`0 \le \operatorname{Im}\zeta \le \tfrac12`
with bounded functions $`k_\zeta(\Delta)` of the modular operator and cuts them off with spectral
projections. The cutoff lemma `stripPower_cutoff` is stated for $`\zeta` in the strip only, where
$`k_\zeta` is bounded; its `**Restriction:**` line records that the statement for all $`\zeta`
needs an unbounded functional calculus composed with a bounded projection. The proof uses only the
strip.

