## Covariant vacuum representations

{docstring Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsCovariantVacuumRep}

Under weak additivity in the von Neumann sense, the vacuum is cyclic and separating for the
algebra of every wedge
([thrm:wedge-cyclic-separating](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:wedge-cyclic-separating)).
Cyclicity comes from the Reeh–Schlieder theorem, since every wedge contains a nonempty diamond;
separation comes from cyclicity for the algebra of the opposite wedge, which lies in the
commutant by locality.

```lean (name := checkWedgeCS)
open Physicslib4 AQFT HaagKastler HaagKastlerNet in
#check
  IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_isCyclicSeparatingVector
```

```leanOutput checkWedgeCS
Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_isCyclicSeparatingVector.{u_1, u_2}
  {H : Type u_1} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {N : HaagKastlerNet}
  {π : N.quasilocal.carrier →⋆ₐ[ℂ] H →L[ℂ] H} {Ω : H} {U : InhomogeneousLorentzGroup → H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H}
  (h : N.IsCovariantVacuumRep π Ω U) (hwa : N.IsWeaklyAdditiveVonNeumann π) :
  (∀ (g : InhomogeneousLorentzGroup),
      IsCyclicSeparatingVector (N.regionVonNeumann π (g • rightWedge)) Ω ∧
        IsCyclicSeparatingVector (N.regionVonNeumann π (g • leftWedge)) Ω) ∧
    ∀ (W : Set StandardMinkowskiSpacetime.Carrier),
      IsWedge W → IsVonNeumannSet (N.regionVonNeumann π W) ∧ IsCyclicSeparatingVector (N.regionVonNeumann π W) Ω
```

## Borchers' theorem

{docstring Physicslib4.Modular.IsHalfSidedTranslation}

The theorem
([thrm:borchers](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:borchers))
states both relations as identities of bounded operators. $`\Delta^{is}` is
`modularUnitaryGroup hM hΩ C hC s`, $`J T J` is `modularConjugationConj`, and $`V(t)` is the
unitary `V t` as a bounded operator.

```lean (name := checkBorchers)
open Physicslib4 Modular in
#check borchers
```

```leanOutput checkBorchers
Physicslib4.Modular.borchers.{u_1} {H : Type u_1} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H]
  {M : Set (H →L[ℂ] H)} {Ω : H} (hM : IsVonNeumannSet M) (hΩ : IsCyclicSeparatingVector M Ω) (C : H ≃ₗᵢ⋆[ℂ] H)
  (hC : ∀ (x : H), C (C x) = x) {V : ℝ → H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H} (hV : IsHalfSidedTranslation M Ω V) :
  (∀ (s t : ℝ),
      (modularUnitaryGroup hM hΩ C hC s).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
          (V t).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
            (modularUnitaryGroup hM hΩ C hC (-s)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
        (V (Real.exp (-(2 * Real.pi * s)) * t)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap) ∧
    ∀ (t : ℝ),
      modularConjugationConj hM hΩ C hC (V t).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
        (V (-t)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap
```

## The wedge corollary

{docstring Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.wedgeReflection}

For a covariant vacuum representation in which $`(R(W_R), \Omega)` is standard
([thrm:wedge-modular-translations](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:wedge-modular-translations)),
the wedge modular group acts on the translations as the boost $`b(-2\pi s)` does, and the modular
conjugation as the wedge reflection. The version for every wedge $`gW_R`,
`IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_modular_translations_general`, conjugates by $`U(g)`.

```lean (name := checkWedgeMT)
open Physicslib4 AQFT HaagKastler HaagKastlerNet in
#check
  IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_modular_translations
```

```leanOutput checkWedgeMT
Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedge_modular_translations.{u_1, u_2} {H : Type u_1}
  [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {N : HaagKastlerNet}
  {π : N.quasilocal.carrier →⋆ₐ[ℂ] H →L[ℂ] H} {Ω : H} {U : InhomogeneousLorentzGroup → H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H}
  (h : N.IsCovariantVacuumRep π Ω U) (hΩ : IsCyclicSeparatingVector (N.regionVonNeumann π rightWedge) Ω)
  (C : H ≃ₗᵢ⋆[ℂ] H) (hC : ∀ (x : H), C (C x) = x) (a : SpacetimeModel) (s : ℝ) :
  (modularUnitaryGroup ⋯ hΩ C hC s).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
        (U (translationSub a)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
          (modularUnitaryGroup ⋯ hΩ C hC (-s)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
      (U (translationSub ((boostMatrix (-(2 * Real.pi * s))) a))).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∧
    (U (translationSub ((boostMatrix (-(2 * Real.pi * s))) a))).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
        (U (wedgeBoost (-(2 * Real.pi * s)))).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
          (U (translationSub a)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∘SL
            (U (wedgeBoost (-(2 * Real.pi * s)))).symm.toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap ∧
      modularConjugationConj ⋯ hΩ C hC (U (translationSub a)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
        (U (translationSub (wedgeReflection a))).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap
```

{docstring Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.wedgeModularDefect}

Without further hypotheses, $`Z` is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group with
$`\Delta_{W_R}^{it} = Z(t)U(b(-2\pi t))`; it fixes $`\Omega`, preserves $`R(W_R)` and commutes
with every translation and every boost of $`W_R`
(`IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedgeModularDefect_properties`,
[crllr:wedge-modular-defect](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#crllr:wedge-modular-defect)).

## Bisognano–Wichmann up to an internal symmetry

{docstring Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsWedgeModularCovariant}

{docstring Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsInternalSymmetry}

The theorem
([thrm:bisognano-wichmann-internal](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:bisognano-wichmann-internal))
assumes (MC) as `hMC` and concludes, for every $`t`, the factorisation of $`\Delta_{W_R}^{it}`,
that $`Z(t)` is an internal symmetry, and that it commutes with the translations, together with
the action of $`J_{W_R}` on the translations. Its docstring carries the `**Restriction:**` line
naming exact Bisognano–Wichmann as the intended form.

```lean (name := checkBWa)
open Physicslib4 AQFT HaagKastler HaagKastlerNet in
#check
  IsCovariantVacuumRep.bisognanoWichmann_internal
```

```leanOutput checkBWa
Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsCovariantVacuumRep.bisognanoWichmann_internal.{u_1, u_2} {H : Type u_1}
  [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {N : HaagKastlerNet}
  {π : N.quasilocal.carrier →⋆ₐ[ℂ] H →L[ℂ] H} {Ω : H} {U : InhomogeneousLorentzGroup → H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H}
  (h : N.IsCovariantVacuumRep π Ω U) (hΩ : IsCyclicSeparatingVector (N.regionVonNeumann π rightWedge) Ω)
  (C : H ≃ₗᵢ⋆[ℂ] H) (hC : ∀ (x : H), C (C x) = x) (hMC : N.IsWedgeModularCovariant π hΩ C hC) :
  (∀ (t : ℝ),
      (∀ (ψ : H),
          (modularUnitaryGroup ⋯ hΩ C hC t) ψ =
            (N.wedgeModularDefect π U hΩ C hC t) ((U (wedgeBoost (-(2 * Real.pi * t)))) ψ)) ∧
        N.IsInternalSymmetry π Ω (N.wedgeModularDefect π U hΩ C hC t) ∧
          ∀ (a : SpacetimeModel) (ψ : H),
            (N.wedgeModularDefect π U hΩ C hC t) ((U (translationSub a)) ψ) =
              (U (translationSub a)) ((N.wedgeModularDefect π U hΩ C hC t) ψ)) ∧
    ∀ (a : SpacetimeModel),
      modularConjugationConj ⋯ hΩ C hC (U (translationSub a)).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap =
        (U (translationSub (wedgeReflection a))).toLinearIsometry.toContinuousLinearMap
```

The corollary `IsCovariantVacuumRep.wedgeModularDefect_commute_wedge_modular`
([crllr:bw-defect-commutes-wedge-modular](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#crllr:bw-defect-commutes-wedge-modular))
adds that $`Z(t)` commutes with the modular group and the modular conjugation of every region
$`W` for which $`\Omega` is cyclic and separating, in particular of every wedge, since an internal
symmetry preserves every region algebra and a unitary that preserves $`\mathcal{M}` and fixes
$`\Omega` commutes with its modular objects.

# A tour of the proof
%%%
tag := "borchers-wedges-tour"
%%%

_Borchers' theorem, by Florig's argument._ The proof follows M. Florig, "On Borchers' theorem",
_Lett. Math. Phys._ 46 (1998) 289–293, in the form of Theorem 2.2.1 of R. Longo's _Lectures on
Conformal Nets_, carried out directly for the von Neumann algebra. For self-adjoint
$`x \in \mathcal{M}`, $`y \in \mathcal{M}'` and $`\tau \ge 0`, the function
$$`F(z) = \big\langle \Delta^{-i\bar z}y\Omega,\ V(\tau e^{2\pi z})\,\Delta^{-iz}x\Omega \big\rangle, \qquad 0 \le \operatorname{Im} z \le \tfrac12,`
is bounded and continuous on the strip and holomorphic inside it, since
$`\operatorname{Im}(\tau e^{2\pi z}) \ge 0` there. Three ingredients make this precise.

* The positive-energy group continues to the closed upper half-plane: with $`P` its positive
  generator, $`\widetilde V(w) = \varepsilon_w(P)`, $`\varepsilon_w(\lambda) = \mathbf{1}_{[0,\infty)}(\lambda)e^{iw\lambda}`,
  is a contraction extending $`V`, holomorphic for $`\operatorname{Im} w > 0` and strongly
  continuous up to the real axis (`Physicslib4/AQFT/PositiveEnergyHalfPlane.lean`).
* The powers $`\Delta^{-iz}` on the strip are written with the bounded functions $`k_\zeta(\Delta)`
  and approximated by the cutoffs $`F_n` with the spectral projections of $`\Delta` on
  $`[1/n, n]`, as in the proof of Takesaki's theorem; the $`F_n` converge uniformly on the strip
  (`borchersApproximant_tendstoUniformlyOn`).
* $`F` is real on both edges of the strip: on the lower edge because $`\langle y\Omega, X x\Omega\rangle`
  is real for self-adjoint $`x \in \mathcal{M}`, $`y \in \mathcal{M}'` and suitable unitaries
  $`X`, and on the upper edge by the same argument for the reflected group $`J V(-t) J`.

A function bounded and continuous on a strip, holomorphic inside and real on both edges is
constant (`eq_of_real_strip_boundary`): Schwarz reflection across the lower edge and a shift give
a function with equal boundary values on the doubled strip, and the strip Liouville and
uniqueness lemmas of blueprint Section 10.6 apply. Comparing $`F` at $`s`, $`0` and $`\tfrac{i}{2}` gives the dilation and
the reflection relation for $`\tau \ge 0`, matrix coefficient by matrix coefficient; they extend to
all $`t` through $`V(-t) = V(t)^*`.

